     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is this a good offer?\n\n**It is a standard, middle-of-the-band initial offer for a Google L6 PM, but it is not strong enough to justify leaving Meta as a happy IC6.** \n\nHere is why:\n* **You are taking a base salary cut:** Google is offering you $235,000, which is below your current $240,000 base and sits near the midpoint of Google\u2019s public SF range ($183k\u2013$271k).\n* **The Year 1 bump is negligible:** \n  * Meta: ~$526,000 ($240k base + $36k bonus + $250k equity).\n  * Google (Offer): ~$552,000 ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k equity).\n  * *Net gain:* ~$26,000 (~5% increase). A 5% bump is nowhere near enough to offset the risk and friction of resetting your tenure, team capital, and performance track record.\n* **No Sign-on Bonus:** Google routinely uses sign-on bonuses to bridge the gap for passive candidates leaving unvested equity at peer tech companies. Leaving $200k in imminent Meta equity on the table with a $0 sign-on bonus is leaving money on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nYou have near-optimal leverage:\n1. **You were headhunted:** You weren\u2019t looking; they sought you out.\n2. **You are happy at Meta:** You have a strong Best Alternative to a Negotiated Agreement (BATNA).\n3. **Lateral parity:** Meta IC6 and Google L6 are direct equivalents; Google knows what Meta pays top-tier Senior PMs.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (and Target Numbers)\n\nWhen countering, ask for an adjustment across **Base**, **Equity**, and a **Sign-on Bonus**.\n\n| Component | Current Meta | Google Initial Offer | Your Counter / Ask | Realistic Target |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **$260,000** | $245,000 \u2013 $255,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | 15% (~$36k) | 20% ($47,000) | 20% (~$52,000 on new base) | 20% |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | ~$250k/yr | $1,080,000 ($270k/yr) | **$1,280,000** ($320k/yr) | $1,150,000 \u2013 $1,200,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$100,000** | $50,000 \u2013 $75,000 |\n| **Year 1 Total** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **~$732,000** | **~$635,000 \u2013 $675,000** |\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Negotiation\n\nReach out to the recruiter via a quick call (followed by an email summary) using the following framing:\n\n1. **Reiterate enthusiasm for the team and scope:** Make it clear you loved meeting the team and are excited about the role.\n2. **Highlight the economic gap:** Frame your counter around the fact that staying at Meta is financially competitive, your base pay would currently decrease, and you are forfeiting near-term vesting stock.\n3. **Give clear, actionable numbers:** \n\n> *\"I really enjoyed the interviews and am excited about the team\u2019s roadmap in SF. Because Google reached out while I\u2019m in a great spot at Meta, I sat down to compare the numbers carefully.* \n> \n> *Right now, the offer represents a slight decrease in base salary ($240k vs $235k) and roughly a 5% delta in Year 1 total compensation, while walking away from $200k in Meta equity vesting over the next 12 months.*\n> \n> *To make this transition compelling and an easy decision, I am looking for:*\n> * *A base salary of **$260,000** (to reflect my level and avoid a base pay cut).*\n> * *An equity grant of **$1,280,000** over 4 years.*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$100,000** to offset forfeited near-term equity at Meta.*\n> \n> *If we can get to this package, I am ready to sign.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Details to Verify Before Signing\n\n* **Confirm Google's Vesting Schedule:** Google often utilizes front-loaded vesting (e.g., 33% / 33% / 22% / 12% or 38% / 32% / 20% / 10%) rather than standard 25% annual vesting. \n  * If it is front-loaded, your Year 1 and Year 2 equity will be significantly higher than $270k, but Years 3 and 4 will drop steeply.\n* **Refresh Assumptions:** Take recruiter refresh illustrations with a grain of salt. Google equity refreshes depend heavily on individual performance ratings and company performance multipliers. Meta's refresh program for IC6 PMs is similarly structured and often carries higher performance bonus upside.",
