     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Yes, you should strongly consider accepting this offer.** \n\nGetting Google to add a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** and bump your base to match Meta without a formal competing offer from a third company is an **exceptional negotiation outcome**. A $200k sign-on bonus is near the absolute top of the band for an L6 non-executive role.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Numbers Breakdown\n\n| Component | Meta (Current) | Google (Final Offer) | Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $0 |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $48,000 (20%) | +$12,000 |\n| **Year 1 RSUs** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 \u2013 $356,000* | +$20,000 to +$106,000 |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | $200,000 | +$200,000 |\n| **Year 1 Total** | **~$526,000** | **~$758,000 \u2013 $844,000** | **+$232,000 \u2013 $318,000 (+44% to +60%)** |\n| **Years 2\u20134 Avg.** | ~$530,000+ | ~$560,000 \u2013 $620,000+** | +$30,000 to +$70,000/yr |\n\n*\\*Google's standard vesting is often front-loaded (33% Year 1, 33% Year 2, 22% Year 3, 12% Year 4), which would yield ~$356,400 in Year 1 equity rather than an even 25% ($270,000).*  \n*\\*\\*Assumes standard target performance equity refreshes stack on top of base/bonus.*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why This is a Big Win\n\n1. **Guaranteed Cash vs. Market Equity:** You swapped $200,000 of unvested Meta RSUs (which carry stock price volatility risk) for **$200,000 in guaranteed cash** up front.\n2. **Year 1 Windfall:** Earning **~$760k\u2013$840k** in Year 1 significantly de-risks the friction of onboarding and changing teams.\n3. **Resume / Market Value:** Having both **Meta IC6** and **Google L6** on your resume establishes you as an elite Senior PM across the two premier product cultures in Big Tech, setting you up well for future Director/Head of Product roles.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Details to Check Before Signing\n\nBefore you sign the Docusign, verify these three clauses with your recruiter:\n\n1. **Sign-on Payout & Clawback Schedule:**\n   * *Is it paid in Month 1 or split across Year 1 and Year 2?* (Google often pays it in your first or second paycheck).\n   * *What is the clawback term?* (Standard is prorated repayment if you leave voluntarily within 12 or 24 months).\n2. **Vesting Schedule:** Confirm whether your GSU grant vests **monthly/quarterly** and if it follows the front-loaded schedule (**33/33/22/12** vs. **25% even**).\n3. **Start Date Timing:** If you have Meta RSUs vesting in the very near future (e.g., in the next 2\u20134 weeks), pick a start date at Google that lets you capture that next vest before you submit your two weeks' notice.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Final Sanity Check: Culture & Lifestyle\n\nFrom a purely financial perspective, this is a clear green light. The only remaining consideration is product culture fit:\n\n* **Meta PM Culture:** Highly operational, scrappy, high velocity, high performance pressure, metric-driven autonomy.\n* **Google PM Culture:** Consensus-driven, engineering-first, slower execution speed, but generally better work-life balance and psychological safety.\n\nIf you are excited about the team, problem space, and the SF office location, **accept the offer.**",
